1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,502
You can kind of
imagine this, right?

2
00:00:01,502 --> 00:00:02,703
You can see it.

3
00:00:02,703 --> 00:00:05,206
I've frozen the top
because of cooling rate.

4
00:00:05,206 --> 00:00:06,740
It's right here.

5
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:08,476
I just cooled the top faster.

6
00:00:08,476 --> 00:00:10,110
The inside isn't cold yet.

7
00:00:10,110 --> 00:00:13,013
It starts to cool, and
it tries to solidify

8
00:00:13,013 --> 00:00:14,048
at a different volume.

9
00:00:14,048 --> 00:00:15,382
It pulls it in.

10
00:00:15,382 --> 00:00:19,520
But see, that changes
the mechanical properties

11
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:21,055
of the surface.

12
00:00:21,055 --> 00:00:22,656
That's called tempering.

13
00:00:22,656 --> 00:00:24,625
That's called tempered glass.

14
00:00:24,625 --> 00:00:28,629
This is tempered glass, and
there is no better example

15
00:00:28,629 --> 00:00:34,201
of temporal glass than the
Prince Rupert's drop, which is

16
00:00:34,201 --> 00:00:36,971
the demo that Peter has come.

17
00:00:36,971 --> 00:00:37,671
Here's Peter.

18
00:00:37,671 --> 00:00:40,174
There's Peter
blowing some glass.

19
00:00:40,174 --> 00:00:43,143
This is what he does
in his free time.

20
00:00:43,143 --> 00:00:45,713
He just throws these big
glass-bowling parties.

21
00:00:45,713 --> 00:00:47,948
He also happens to be the
director of the glass lab,

22
00:00:47,948 --> 00:00:49,683
like I said.

23
